WHAT THIS MEANS IN PRACTICE

Not legal advice, and the terms above are what actually govern. This is here
because a licence nobody understands is a licence nobody respects.

You may:
  - read all of the source, which is the point, since the client is an
    unsigned binary that reads your hardware and sends a report
  - build it yourself and run it on your own machines
  - test cards you are selling, whether you are an individual or a shop
  - modify it and share your changes
  - use it in teaching, research or anything non-production

You may not:
  - run your own service that issues, signs or verifies GPU certificates

On 2030-08-13 every version released before then becomes Apache 2.0, and
these restrictions end permanently.

Practically speaking, a competing instance would not work anyway. Certificates
are trusted because of the key that signs them, and a copy signs with its own.
This licence exists so the lazy path is also the illegal one, not because the
code is the valuable part.


